verdict=IN-SCOPE: the store and every reference to it are removed as the plan sets out, and the one file outside its Files list is an archived record whose dead citation the commit gate forced
# Judged by a subagent that re-read the plan BY PATH after it was replaced, and
# the staged diff in full.
# 
# Every file in the diff is in the plan's Files list except one, and that one is
# what it was asked to rule on.
# 
# THE 043 EDIT, ruled fallout rather than new scope, and argued rather than
# waved through. `tools/decisions-check.mjs` was read directly: its own comment
# says any record carrying a "## Built already" section has its paths checked,
# top-ranked or not. 043 is archived, its section was written in earlier landed
# work, and it cited the store module the plan explicitly authorises deleting.
# Once that file is gone the citation is dead and the gate refuses the commit —
# there is no discretion in whether to touch 043, only in how honestly. The edit
# drops the path from one bullet, says in a sentence why, and leaves the
# historical content after it verbatim.
# 
# FOUR CLASSES OF CLAIM CHECKED AGAINST THE FILES, all held. Nothing in the tree
# still imports the store: grepped for the module and for all eleven of its
# exported identifiers, zero references in src/ or tools/, the only hit a
# plain-text filename in an explanatory comment. `requestPersistence` is exported
# from src/session.ts and that is the copy src/lensrig.ts imports, so deleting
# the store deletes a duplicate rather than a capability. `deviceNoun` is defined
# and used inside src/platform.ts, so dropping it from main.ts's import is right.
# Nothing is left behind: no dead identifier, no `.kept` style rule, and the
# module map was regenerated rather than hand-edited.
# 
# THE CONTRACT WAS THE PART MOST LIKELY TO ROT AND IT IS CLEAN. `showLoneWithEdit`
# lost its `keptId` parameter, the orphaned second `@returns` that sat between the
# old parameter and `bytes`, and the sentence describing a store row; the
# docstring stacked above it belonged to a deleted function and went with that
# function rather than being left dangling, which is the swallowed-contract shape
# this repository has the most lessons about. `openKeepFile`'s own note about
# ending in a null store id is rewritten too, and the call site matches.
# 
# THE BOOT DELETE IS ONE CALL, unguarded, fire-and-forget, and nothing larger.
# 
# DELTA SINCE THAT JUDGEMENT, and it is the watcher's own finding acted on: 051's
# Built already said main.ts reaches the store "through one import and nine
# places" while the list beneath names seven groups, so the arithmetic did not
# resolve. The count is removed rather than corrected — a number in prose is a
# second answer to a question the list already answers, which is the reasoning the
# hub applied to its own "Twelve gates" heading. Four words, no path, no claim.
# 
# VERIFIED BEFORE COMMITTING, against a served build. a11y-walk went from 131 ok
# with 2 failures to 129 ok with NONE, and a diff of the check names shows the
# only two that disappeared are the `#keptDlg` sweeps at each viewport width — the
# sweep measured everything it measured before, minus the surface that no longer
# exists. keep-walk green, 22.09% painted and 22.09% restored. release-walk all
# checks passed. A scratch harness planted an `ips-kept` database on the origin,
# loaded the app, and found it gone afterwards with the other five stores
# untouched — which is what the boot delete is for, and what checking only that
# the app no longer CREATES one would not have shown.
